  "reconstruction": {
    "scope": "Specific Capela dos Ossos interior and connected São Francisco exterior envelope: traversable entry vestibule/stone portal, three-aisle hall with eight bone-covered piers, pale groin vaults/ribs and original simplified ceiling scrolls, dense original skull/long-bone wall vocabulary, high side openings, dark/pale banded floor and simplified gilded twisted-column altar/cross. Host includes mapped stone nave/pointed west portico, crenellated gable/parapets and conical corner pinnacles, connected white lower ranges/shared tiled roofs. Chapel is an interior allocation inside the named host, not a separate building shell. No copied religious painting, readable inscription or photographic bone atlas; no full host church/museum interior.",
    "identityBasis": "Exact-QID Capela dos Ossos node6763794307/Q2561983 and named standard PMTiles museum point17598949838723 lie in mapped São Francisco host control35184534465311; host name is corroborated by PMTiles church point35184534465311. No separate chapel footprint/interior map exists. Anchor-7.9083714,38.5687532; heading0, internal local+x angle-.19rad follows host axes. Chapel is allocated approximately within the connected southern wing: room11x20.5m along local+z, altar south/local+z, entry via north vestibule and west courtyard access. Internal station/facing allocation is explicitly approximate, not a surveyed interior. Replace the one complete host control while retaining every independent neighboring building.",
    "heightBasis": "Ordinary-picture/map visualization estimates, not surveys: host nave approximately58x22m/eave21.5m/ridge25.2m, west five-bay pointed portico around5.7m deep/7m high, crenellated front/parapet and small conical corner pinnacles. Lower host ranges7.5-9m/eaves with red tiled roofs. Chapel clear room11x20.5m, pier spring3.35m/vault crown5.9m, eight round piers in two rows/five longitudinal bays, original skull/bone and ceiling motif allocations. Vestibule/portal access, exact columns/vault/room dimensions, unseen roofs and ornament inventory remain approximations.",
    "terrainBasis": "Original standard6m Mapzen urban ground/water retained. Host and chapel use local level floors/buried occupied bases, with a short west courtyard approach to the vestibule; no broad city/block slab, flattening or ground/water cutout. Coarse urban DEM does not resolve paving/entrance thresholds or exact interior floor levels. Main nave interior and remaining museum rooms are unmodeled enclosed host context. The chapel interior is real geometry behind its entry, not a roof-removed world-view cutaway.",
    "architecturalAngleRadians": -0.19,
